lar regularity in H005. Conversely the original data produce these hypotheses for every total extension. wDx evaluates the Fréchet derivative on the coordinate unit vector; wT, wLap and the squared Euclidean norms therefore give exactly the prescribed derivatives, componentwise Laplacian and Frobenius density. The conclusion quantifies over every positive real t, asserting Lebesgue integrability, ordinary differentiability and zero derivative of the actual integral, plus equality of energies at every two positive times. Restriction and extension give these conclusions in both directions because derivatives at positive times are local and the density sections and integrals coincide. No condition or assertion at time zero or outside positive times is imposed; the integral has no factor 1/2.\"}}},\"library_terms\":{}}}","status":"completed"}}

